"math/lean/docs/111.md at main · openai/math · GitHub (https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/lean/docs/111.md)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # One-sample matroid prophet inequalities against an almighty adversary\n\n# One-sample matroid prophet inequalities against an almighty adversary\n\nThe following describes the scope of the Lean formalization related to the following accompanying paper(s):\n\n  * One Sample Suffices for Matroid Prophet Inequalities against an Almighty Adversary\n\n## Scope\n\nThe formalization proves a one-sample matroid prophet inequality with expected reward at least $2^{-310}$ times the expected offline optimum. Each element has one independent sample paired with an identically distributed nonnegative online value, and all coordinates are independent. The rule and finite seed law depend only on the labeled matroid; choices are irrevocable and feasible after every prefix. The arrival order may depend measurably on all samples, values, and the seed, and only the offline optimum must be integrable.\n\nA supporting hidden-vector selection result for fixed nonnegative matroid weights gives expected worst-order reward at least $2^{-293}$ times the optimum, again with a finite seed law and prefix feasibility.\n\n## Comparator links\n\nResult  | Comparator statement\n--- | ---\nOne-sample matroid prophet inequality against an almighty adversary  | MatroidProphet.lean\nHidden-vector matroid selection with worst-order guarantee  | MatroidSecretary.lean--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatroid Secretary Is Equivalent to Contention Resolution (https://arxiv.org/abs/2103.04205)\nciteturn4academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Specifically, the matroid secretary conjecture is true if and only if every matroid admits an online random-order contention resolution scheme which, given an arbitrary (possibly correlated) prior distribution over subsets of the ground set, matches the balance ratio of the best offline scheme for that distribution up to a constant. ... First, we use duality arguments to reduce the matroid secretary problem to the matroid prophet secretary problem with arbitrarily correlated distributions.\nTitle: Matroid Secretary Is Equivalent to Contention Resolution\nAuthors: Shaddin Dughmi\nDate: Sat Mar  6 22:46:29 2021\n\nWe show that the matroid secretary problem is equivalent to correlated contention resolution in the online random-order model. Specifically, the matroid secretary conjecture is true if and only if every matroid admits an online random-order contention resolution scheme which, given an arbitrary (possibly correlated) prior distribution over subsets of the ground set, matches the balance ratio of the best offline scheme for that distribution up to a constant. We refer to such a scheme as universal. Our result indicates that the core challenge of the matroid secretary problem lies in resolving contention for positively correlated inputs, in particular when the positive correlation is benign in as much as offline contention resolution is concerned. Our result builds on our previous work which establishes one direction of this equivalence, namely that the secretary conjecture implies universal random-order contention resolution, as well as a weak converse, which derives a matroid secretary algorithm from a random-order contention resolution scheme with only partial knowledge of the distribution. It is this weak converse that we strengthen in this paper: We show that universal random-order contention resolution for matroids, in the usual setting of a fully known prior distribution, suffices to resolve the matroid secretary conjecture in the affirmative. Our proof is the composition of three reductions. First, we use duality arguments to reduce the matroid secretary problem to the matroid prophet secretary problem with arbitrarily correlated distributions. Second, we introduce a generalization of contention resolution we term labeled contention resolution, to which we reduce the correlated matroid prophet secretary problem. Finally, we combine duplication of elements with limiting arguments to reduce labeled contention resolution to classical contention resolution.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Matroid Secretary Conjecture is True | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2609.14555)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The paper establishes a constant-factor guarantee for this problem for every matroid, resolving the matroid secretary conjecture in its original random-order setting. ... The paper also describes consequences for submodular maximization under matroid constraints, single-sample matroid prophet inequalities, and intersections of multiple matroids.\nSubmitted 13 Sept 2026\n\nen\n\n# The Matroid Secretary Conjecture is True\n\nGeorgia Tech\n\nSS\n\nSahil Singla\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe resolve the matroid secretary conjecture, giving an online algorithm that accepts each element of the offline optimum with probability at least $1/4$1/4. The algorithm only needs the number of elements in advance and independence-oracle access to subsets of already-arrived elements; it does not need to know the matroid upfront.\n\n## AI Overview\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe matroid secretary problem asks an online algorithm to select valuable items that arrive in a random order. Each item must be accepted or rejected immediately, and the accepted items must remain feasible under a matroid constraint. The paper establishes a constant-factor guarantee for this problem for every matroid, resolving the matroid secretary conjecture in its original random-order setting.\n\nThe key construction converts the online process into a carefully simulated two-sided sampling problem. The figure below illustrates this conversion: an initially discarded prefix supplies virtual sample information, while the remaining items are treated as actual arrivals.\n\nImage: Illustration of the virtual order construction, in which a discarded prefix is interleaved with remaining actual arrivals.\n\nFigure 1: The reduction from an actual arrival order to an independent virtual order and sample assignment.\n\nThe result is a 4-competitive algorithm for arbitrary matroids. More strongly, every element in a particular offline optimum is accepted with probability at least $1/4$1/4. This per-element guarantee implies the overall expected-value guarantee, while also revealing how the algorithm preserves individual members of the offline solution.\n\n## The Problem and Its Context\n\nA matroid is a system of feasible subsets with two basic properties: removing elements from a feasible set keeps it feasible, and if one feasible set is larger than another, an element from the larger set can be added to the smaller one while preserving feasibility. Examples include selecting at most a fixed number of items, choosing a forest in a graph, or selecting representatives subject to partition constraints.\n\nMatroids are important because weighted greedy selection is optimal for them. If all item values were known in advance, sorting items by value and adding each item whenever independence was preserved would produce an optimal solution.\n\nThe secretary version removes this offline knowledge. Items arrive in uniformly random order, their values are revealed only on arrival, and decisions are irrevocable. The algorithm is given the number of items $n$n, but it does not know the matroid representation in advance. It may query an independence oracle, but only on elements that have already arrived.\n\nThe conjecture proposed that every matroid has an online algorithm with a constant competitive ratio independent of its rank $r$r. Before this paper, the best general guarantee was $O(\\log\\log r)$O(lo g lo g r)-competitive. Earlier work had successively obtained $O(\\log r)$O(lo g r) and $O(\\sqrt{\\log r})$O(lo g r​) bounds. Constant guarantees were known for special matroid classes, but not for arbitrary matroids in the standard random-order model.\n\nThis setting should be distinguished from prophet inequalities and prophet-secretary problems. Those models often reveal values through independent random variables or permit different arrival assumptions. A result in one of those models does not automatically apply when values are fixed adversarially and only their arrival order is random.\n\n## Main Result and Contributions\n\nThe paper’s main theorem provides an online algorithm that:\n\n  * always returns an independent set;\n  * accepts every element of the canonical offline greedy optimum with probability at least $1/4$1/4;\n  * obtains expected value at least one quarter of the offline optimum;\n  * is therefore 4-competitive;\n  * requires only the known value of $n$n and independence-oracle access;\n  * does not require an explicit description of the matroid; and\n  * is ordinal, relying on comparisons and greedy orderings rather than numerical scaling.\n\nThe contribution differs from prior approaches in three ways. First, it applies to arbitrary matroids rather than a structured subclass. Second, it works in the original random-order secretary model rather than a stronger arrival or information model. Third, it proves a per-element probability guarantee, not only an aggregate expected-value approximation.\n\nThe paper also describes consequences for submodular maximization under matroid constraints, single-sample matroid prophet inequalities, and intersections of multiple matroids. The stated extensions are not the central proof presented here and are deferred to the full version.\n\n## Significance and Open Questions\n\nThe result changes the general guarantee for the matroid secretary problem from a rank-dependent factor to a constant. Its proof isolates a reusable principle: when adaptive decisions make a competitor appear history-dependent, a reversible update can restore the symmetry needed for random-order analysis.\n\nSeveral questions remain open. The constant $4$4 is not shown to be optimal, and the stronger $e$e-competitive form of the conjecture remains unresolved. The paper is theoretical and does not provide an experimental evaluation. Practical performance also depends on the cost of repeatedly computing greedy solutions and making oracle queries. Finally, the guarantee assumes uniformly random arrivals, fixed values, known $n$n, and exact matroid independence. Under these assumptions, however, the paper provides a complete constant-competitiveness result for the original conjecture.\n\nMatroids, secretary problems, and online mechanisms\n\nThis paper introduced the matroid secretary problem and formulated the conjecture that every matroid admits an O(1)-competitive online algorithm. It is therefore the foundational reference that the present paper resolves.\n\n[BIK07] Moshe Babaioff, Nicole Immorlica, and Robert Kleinberg. Matroids, secretary problems, and online mechanisms. In Proceedings of the 18th Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA), pages 434–443, 2007.\n\nMatroid secretary problems\n\nThis journal treatment developed the general matroid secretary framework and established important prior guarantees, including the O(log r) bound. The present result explicitly identifies itself as resolving the conjecture from this line of work.\n\n[BIKK18] Moshe Babaioff, Nicole Immorlica, David Kempe, and Robert Kleinberg. Matroid secretary problems. Journal of the ACM, 65(6):35:1–35:26, 2018.\n\nA simple O(log log(rank))-competitive algorithm for the matroid secretary problem\n\nThis was the best general competitive guarantee for the original matroid secretary problem for more than a decade, prior to the claimed constant-competitive resolution. It provides the key benchmark against which the new theorem is compared.\n\n[FSZ15] Moran Feldman, Ola Svensson, and Rico Zenklusen. A simple O(log log(rank))-competitive algorithm for the matroid secretary problem. In Proceedings of the 26th Annual ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA), pages 1189–1201, 2015.\n\nThe two-sided game of googol and sample-based prophet inequalities\n\nThe paper’s central proof strategy is based on a matroid version of the random-order two-sided Game-of-Googol. This citation supplies the conceptual model that is adapted into the new reversible-sample algorithm.\n\n[CCES20] José R. Correa, Andrés Cristi, Boris Epstein, and José A. Soto. The two-sided game of googol and sample-based prophet inequalities. In Proceedings of the 2020 ACM-SIAM Symposium on Discrete Algorithms (SODA), pages 2066–2081. SIAM, 2020.\n\nComments on bases in dependence structures\n\nBrualdi’s symmetric basis-exchange theorem underlies the paper’s one-coordinate sensitivity lemma, which shows that changing one weight can alter the greedy optimum by at most one exchange partner. This structural fact is essential to the algorithm’s 1/4 per-element guarantee.\n\n[Bru69] Richard A. Brualdi. Comments on bases in dependence structures. Bulletin of the Australian Mathematical Society, 1(2):161–167, 1969.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{singla2026matroidsecretaryconjecturetrue, title={The Matroid Secretary Conjecture is True}, author={Sahil Singla}, year={2026}, eprint={2609.14555}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.DS}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14555}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatroid Secretary Conjecture and Beyond: Proving Competitive Ratios (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/2609.19118)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The resulting simulated arrival order is uniform and independent of the activation bits, exactly satisfying the prophet algorithm’s order requirement. ... In particular, the paper explicitly contrasts this result with an independent concurrent result giving a $1/64$6 general-matroid secretary guarantee, while emphasizing that the present work obtains the optimal $1/64$7 constant for linear matroids and the tight $1/64$8 single-sample prophet constant. ... This is called the strong matroid secretary conjecture.\n## Reduction from prophet inequalities to secretary algorithms\n\nThe paper then converts the single-sample prophet result into a secretary guarantee for arbitrary matroids. A direct approach would use an initial rejected prefix as the prophet samples, but this creates an implementation problem: the simulated prophet algorithm may later select an element from the rejected prefix, which cannot be accepted in the physical secretary process.\n\nThe reduction resolves this through three steps.\n\nFirst, a random half-sample is rejected and used to filter the remaining elements. An element is retained as eligible if it is not spanned by the earlier sampled elements in the greedy weight order. If $\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 4 denotes the eligible set, then\n\n$\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 5\n\nThe first inequality controls the total weight that can be lost through unimplementable selections; the second ensures that a constant fraction of the optimum remains available.\n\nSecond, each eligible element is activated independently with probability $\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 6. The simulated prophet sample and actual value are independently activated versions of the element’s secretary weight. A useful prophet selection has expected contribution linear in $\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 7, whereas an unimplementable selection requires both sample and actual activation and therefore incurs only a quadratic $\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 8 loss.\n\nThird, the reduction interleaves the stored sample prefix with the physical arrivals. The resulting simulated arrival order is uniform and independent of the activation bits, exactly satisfying the prophet algorithm’s order requirement. The coupling is exact, not an approximation based on asymptotic random-order arguments.\n\nThe resulting guarantee is\n\n$\\alpha=1/2$α=1/2 9\n\nChoosing $1/64$0 gives an $1/64$1 secretary ratio. For $1/64$2, the paper obtains:\n\n> Every matroid admits a polynomial-time ordinal $1/64$3-competitive secretary algorithm.\n\nThe reduction preserves ordinal information because all comparisons used by the simulated prophet algorithm are comparisons among already revealed secretary weights, artificial zeros, and fixed tie priorities. \nInstead of merely counting how many objects have been accepted, the algorithm checks every possible subspace—such as a line, plane, or higher-dimensional region—and keeps the expected amount of accepted span inside that region under control.\n\nThis is important because an accepted set with only a few objects might still block an important future object if all those objects point in similar directions.\n\nTo make this work, the authors use:\n\n  * Finite linear programs to decide how much probability to place on accepting each object.\n  * Uncrossing, a method for simplifying overlapping mathematical constraints.\n  * A separation or minimax argument, which shows that all the constraints can be satisfied at the same time.\n\nThe resulting algorithm uses only the relative order of observed values, so it is ordinal.\n\n### Studying the single-sample prophet problem\n\nIn the prophet problem, each object has a random value. The algorithm does not know the value distributions, but it receives one independent sample from each distribution before the real objects arrive.\n\nThe authors design an algorithm that maintains a temporary “sample world.” When a real value appears, it carefully updates this temporary world while deciding whether to accept the object.\n\nThe key idea is that the final accepted set behaves exactly like this:\n\n  1. Find the best legal set in a fresh random world.\n  2. Keep each object in that set independently with probability $1/2$.\n\nThis is called a fair thinning of the optimum.\n\n## 4. What did the paper find?\n\nThe main results are summarized below.\n\nSetting  | Guarantee  | Meaning\n--- | --- | ---\nSecretary problem for linear matroids  | $1/e \\approx 37\\%$1/e≈37%  | Gets at least about 37% of the best possible value\nSingle-sample prophet problem for all matroids  | $1/2 = 50\\%$1/2=50%  | Gets half of the expected optimum\nSecretary problem for all matroids  | $1/64 \\approx 1.56\\%$1/64≈1.56%  | Gives a positive constant guarantee for every matroid\n\n### Result 1: The conjecture is proved for linear matroids\n\nFor every linear matroid, the authors give an ordinal algorithm that selects each element of the optimal solution with the same probability as the classic secretary algorithm selects the best applicant.\n\nAs the number of objects grows, this probability approaches\n\n$\\frac{1}{e} \\approx 0.3679.$e 1​≈0.3679.\n\nThis is important because $1/e$ is already the best possible guarantee in the ordinary one-choice problem. Therefore, no algorithm can guarantee a better universal constant for every matroid.\n\nThe result applies to many important structures, including:\n\n  * Graph and network problems, through graphic matroids.\n  * Regular matroids.\n  * Laminar matroids.\n  * Transversal matroids.\n  * Gammoids.\n  * Uniform and partition matroids.\n\n### Result 2: Every matroid has a constant guarantee\n\nFor completely general matroids, the authors give a polynomial-time ordinal secretary algorithm with guarantee\n\n$\\frac{1}{64}.$64 1​.\n\nThis means that, no matter how complicated the matroid is, the algorithm can obtain at least $1/64$ of the best possible expected value.\n\nThis is weaker than $1/e$, but it is still significant. Before this work, researchers did not know whether every matroid had any fixed positive guarantee at all.\n\n### Result 3: One sample is enough for a tight prophet guarantee\n\nFor every matroid, one independent sample per object is enough to obtain a $1/2$ guarantee in the prophet setting.\n\nThe result is especially strong because the algorithm’s output has an exact interpretation: it is like taking the best possible solution and independently keeping each selected object with probability one-half.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Contention Resolution to Matroid Secretary and Back∗ (https://www.shaddin.com/papers/crs_sec_combined.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Specifically, the matroid secretary conjecture is true if andonly if every matroid admits an online random-order contention resolution scheme which, given ... isolates a technical hurdle: from the secretary problem to the (correlated) prophet secretary\nFrom Contention Resolution to Matroid Secretary and Back∗\nShaddin Dughmi†\nDepartment of Computer Science\nUniversity of Southern California\nshaddin@usc.edu\nDecember 9, 2024\nAbstract\nWe show that the matroid secretary problem is equivalent to correlated contention resolution\nin the online random-order model. Specifically, the matroid secretary conjecture is true if and\nonly if every matroid admits an online random-order contention resolution scheme which, given\nan arbitrary (possibly correlated) prior distribution over subsets of the ground set, matches the\nbalance ratio of the best offline scheme for that distribution up to a constant.\nIntegral to our result is a polyhedral characterization of the (correlated) distributions per-\nmitting offline contention resolution over a given matroid — we refer to these distributions as\nuncontentious. Our characterization can be viewed as a distributional generalization of the ma-\ntroid covering theorem, and isolates the kind and degree of positive correlation that is benign\nfor offline contention resolution. Using this characterization, we are able to show that the set\nof improving elements for a subsample of a weighted matroid is uncontentious — a fact that\nserves as a key technical component of our result.\nOne direction of our equivalence is relatively straightforward: a competitive secretary algo-\nrithm yields a random-order contention resolution scheme — one which approximately matches\nthe best possible offline balance ratio — by providing an approximate solution to its dual. The\nother direction is more technical, and involves a composition of three reductions which each\nisolates a technical hurdle: from the secretary problem to the (correlated) prophet secretary\nproblem, then from that to a labeled generalization of (random-order) contention resolution,\nand finally from labeled contention resolution to its unlabeled counterpart. The uncontentious-\nness of the set of improving elements implies that the resulting contention resolution problem\nfeatures an (offline) uncontentious distribution, which therefore implies our main result.\nOne interpretation of our result is that handling the positive correlation inherent to uncon-\ntentious distributions is the key technical barrier to resolving the matroid secretary conjecture.\n1\nIntroduction\nThis paper follows in the hallowed tradition in theoretical computer science of reducing the number\nof questions without providing any answers. We establish an equivalence between one of the central\nopen problems in online algorithm design, the matroid secretary conjecture, and the increasingly rich\nand fruitful framework of contention resolution. Specifically, we show that the matroid secretary\nproblem admits a constant-competitive algorithm if and only if matroid contention resolution for\ngeneral (correlated) distributions is approximately as powerful (up to a constant) in the online\n∗This journal paper unifies and expands upon results that span two conference publications: [13] and [14].\n†This work was supported by NSF CAREER Award CCF-1350900 and NSF Grant CCF-2009060.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) On the Strong Matroid Secretary Conjecture and Beyond (https://www.researchgat